# fixedbugs, defect 584, lane b19-dep, 2026-10-10 (panel 208's R1).
#
# SYMPTOM: `sprintf` from `stdio.h`, called, with the program's own header
# defining `_FORTIFY_SOURCE 0`: the call names the C library's declaration,
# which this Mac's SDK marks `deprecated`, and clang printed its warning at
# the emitted C of the call. Fortified, the call is a macro naming
# `__builtin___sprintf_chk`, and was silent (panel 208's two seats).
#
# FIX: `-Wdeprecated-declarations`, `-Wdeprecated-pragma` and
# `-Wattribute-warning` are ignored over the whole unit after the groups'
# close, the program's own lines as the compiler's (`emit/deprecation.hero`,
# panel 208's R1): a deprecation is advice about a program, not a mistake in
# it, and this language has no warning level.
#
# **Since 2026-11-10** (panel 243's R1, defect 2421) a binding declares no
# parameter past a header's `...`, and `sprintf(buf: ptr, format: cstr lent,
# n: i64)` is refused on `n`. The call is the header's door, `print_number`,
# a function of a new name with a fixed prototype holding the literal format,
# which quiets the deprecation of its own call, since a header's own code is
# outside the quiet above; the compiler's lines name the door and never
# `sprintf`.

extern "fixedbugs-584-sprintf-with-fortification-off-builds-silent.h"
    function malloc(n: u64) -> ptr
    function free(p: ptr)
    function print_number(buf: ptr, n: i64) -> i32

function main()
    p = malloc(64.to_u64().must())
    k = print_number(p, 42)
    print(k)
    free(p)
